<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.5.3: Examining an Equation in Two Variables and Its Graph, Part 1</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00132</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.5.3: Examining an Equation in Two Variables and Its Graph, Part 1</md:title>
    <md:uuid>40283ffc-ea80-4fd6-97a2-4ae4f3d107de</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>To get snacks for a class trip, Clare went to the “bulk” section of the grocery store, where she could buy any quantity of a product and the prices are usually good.</para>
      <para>Clare purchased some salted almonds at $6 a pound and some dried figs at $9 per pound. She spent $75 before tax.</para>
      <div>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Bowl of figs and almonds.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_07_06_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="58b7491f-9daa-42f2-808a-e8da96a4cf85" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>If she bought 2 pounds of almonds, how many pounds of figs did she buy?
        </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 7</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 7.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="7bbc8bae-d928-47af-a361-13d9354c1358" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>If she bought 1 pound of figs, how many pounds of almonds did she buy?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 11</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 11.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Q3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an <term data-store="storename">equation</term> that describes the relationship between pounds of figs and pounds of almonds that Clare bought and the dollar amount that she paid. Be sure to specify what the <term data-store="storename">variables</term> represent.</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(6a+9f=75\)
      \(a\): pounds of almonds, \(f\): pounds of figs</para>
          <para>For questions 4 and 5, use the graph that represents the quantities in the situation.
    </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>For questions 4 and 5, use the graph that represents the quantities in the situation.
</para>
      <figure>
        <media alt="Graph of 2 intersecting lines and 5 points, origin O, with grid. Almonds (pounds) and dried figs (pounds).">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_07_06_02.svg"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Choose any point on the line, state its coordinates, and explain what it tells us.
      </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      Point A or (2, 7). Clare bought 2 pounds of almonds and 7 pounds of figs for a total of $75.
    </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Choose any point that is not on the line, state its coordinates, and explain what it tells us.
      </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      Point D or \((1, 1)\). Clare bought 1 pound of almonds and 1 pound of figs, and the total was not $75.
    </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
